  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct solution is to set the <b>DeletionPolicy attribute to \"Retain\"</b> in your CloudFormation template for the AWS::RDS::DBInstance resource. This special policy tells CloudFormation to keep the RDS instance intact and not delete it, even when you delete the stack. This makes sure your data stays safe for future reference.</p><p>Let’s look at why the other choices are not suitable:</p><ul><li><i>Configure a Read Replica in the RDS setup to ensure the replica remains available after deleting the stack.</i> – Read Replicas are useful for scaling and high availability, but they do not protect your original DB instance upon stack deletion. Also, CloudFormation will remove replicas created within the template when the stack is deleted unless explicitly told to retain them.</li><li><i>Include a WaitPolicy property with the value \"Retain\" for the AWS::RDS::DBInstance resource in your CloudFormation template.</i> – There is no WaitPolicy property in CloudFormation for RDS resources; thus, this option would not work or provide the desired behavior.</li><li><i>Change the DBInstanceClass property of the AWS::RDS::DBInstance to a read-only type in the template.</i> – The DBInstanceClass property determines the instance size/type, not its data retention policy. It doesn't affect what happens when a stack is deleted and will not preserve your database.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> Use the DeletionPolicy \"Retain\" on resources you want to keep after deleting a stack, especially for databases and persistent storage!</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your team needs to use a CloudFormation template for application testing. There’s an important requirement: if the stack is deleted, the associated database should not be removed, as it must be kept for future analysis. With CloudFormation, what’s the correct approach to meet this need?</p>",
